Stage 3 deliberately has **no** `synthdefs/ji_freqset_receiver.scd`; the OSC
receiver in `test_bridge.scd` supersedes the old server-buffer lookup. The
12-slot Hz Buffer mirror is written for future stages but is not read by any
SynthDef yet.

Stage 4 likewise has **no** `synthdefs/ji_autotune_dual.scd`: it starts two
instances of the verified `\ji_autotune_ch` graph, one per input channel, so
there is no second autotune DSP definition to maintain.

Stage 5 adds **no** SynthDef file and no Python/OSC code: it changes
`startup.scd` to the separate-device 2-in/16-out configuration, adds
`test_ableton_routing.scd`, `config/ableton_routing.json`, and
`README_stage5.md`. Stage 4 audio stays on output pair 0/1; the remaining pairs
are reserved.

Stage 5.5 adds **no** SynthDef or Python/OSC code: `test_live_rig.scd` reuses
the verified Stage 4 orchestration and receiver, moves the loop outputs to
SC 2/3, and maps the two live channels to SC 0/1. It adds
`config/live_rig_routing.json` and `README_live_rig.md`.

### 4.4 Startup order (Stage 5 onward)

1. Human verifies MiniFuse 2 and BlackHole 16ch exist at 48 kHz (Audio MIDI Setup).
2. Human opens Ableton, configures input as BlackHole 16ch (inputs 1–16) and output as MiniFuse 2/headphones.
3. Human launches SC IDE, evaluates `startup.scd` (server boots with separate in/out devices, buses allocated, SynthDefs loaded).
4. Human evaluates `test_ableton_routing.scd` (Stage 5 infrastructure), then `test_live_rig.scd` (Stage 5.5 product), `test_autotune.scd` (Stage 1), `test_bridge.scd` (Stage 3), or `test_dual_loop.scd` (Stage 4).
5. Human launches the Python bridge (`python control/ji_bridge.py`); add `--midi` for the Stage 6 MPE rig after IAC/Ableton setup.
6. Stage 7: evaluate `ui/control_surface.scd` after `test_live_rig.scd`, then launch the minimal GUI:
   `uv run --with python-osc --with PyQt6 python ui/ji_ui.py`.
7. Human keeps the GUI open for the set; closing it must not stop the audio/MIDI rig.

---

## 5. Global Contract

This contract applies to **all stages**. Included verbatim in every agent prompt.

### 5.1 Naming conventions

| Entity | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| SynthDef name | `\ji_` prefix, camelCase | `\ji_autotune_ch` |
| Global variable | `~ji_*` | `~ji_autotune_freq1` |
| Control bus | `~ji_<subsystem>_<param>` | `~ji_tartini_freq1` |
| Audio bus | `~ji_audio_*` | `~ji_audio_loop1` |
| Buffer | `~ji_buf_*` | `~ji_buf_loop1` |
| SynthDef argument | camelCase | `retuneTime`, `inBus` |
| OSC address | `/ji/<subsystem>/<action>` | `/ji/mutation/trigger` |
| Python module | `snake_case.py` | `mutation.py` |
| Python class | `PascalCase` | `MutationEngine` |
| MIDI map file | `config/*.json` | `config/midi_map.json` |

### 5.2 Bus allocation (cumulative, never reassign)

| Bus | Name | Stage | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | `~ji_tartini_freq1` | 0 | Debug tracker freq, ch 0 |
| 1 | `~ji_tartini_freq2` | 0 | Debug tracker freq, ch 1 |
| 2 | `~ji_tartini_hasfreq1` | 0 | Debug confidence, ch 0 |
| 3 | `~ji_tartini_hasfreq2` | 0 | Debug confidence, ch 1 |
| 4 | `~ji_cpu` | 0 | System CPU |
| 5 | `~ji_autotune_freq1` | 1 | Autotune tracker freq, ch 0 |
| 6 | `~ji_autotune_hasfreq1` | 1 | Autotune confidence, ch 0 |
| 7 | `~ji_autotune_target1` | 1 | Target freq, ch 0 |
| 8 | `~ji_autotune_freq2` | 4 | Autotune tracker freq, ch 1 |
| 9 | `~ji_autotune_hasfreq2` | 4 | Autotune confidence, ch 1 |
| 10 | `~ji_autotune_target2` | 4 | Target freq, ch 1 |
| 11–14 | `~ji_loop_freq1..4` | 4 | Loop tracker freqs |
| 15–18 | `~ji_loop_hasfreq1..4` | 4 | Loop confidences |

**Audio bus allocation:** Stage 4 keeps 0–1 input and 0–1 output (SC reuses indices); mono loops sum to output 0 or 1, so no internal loop audio buses are allocated. Stage 5 sets 2 input / 16 output bus channels; output buses 0–15 are the eight stereo pairs routed to BlackHole. No internal loop audio buses are needed. VST/MPE do not consume global audio bus indices.

### 5.3 Audio-thread rule (inviolable)

**Never modify a running Synth's internal structure at runtime.** Structural changes only via SynthDef recompilation + node replacement with crossfade, or allocating a new node and freeing the old one. Parameter changes via `Synth.set` and buffer content via `Buffer.setn` are safe.

### 5.4 MIDI-thread rule

MPE MIDI output must not block the audio thread or the main control thread. The arp runs in its own daemon thread; the drone sends from the bridge callback. The arp thread owns a `threading.Lock` around the current pattern and a `threading.Event` for stop. Both voices receive immutable engine snapshots from the bridge; they never read the `MutationEngine` directly. Pitch-bend calculation happens in the control layer, never in a SynthDef. `mido` is imported lazily and only when `--midi` opens a real port.

### 5.5 Output format (for agent-generated code)

1. Fenced code blocks per file, each preceded by `### FILE: <path>`.
2. `### SELF-REVIEW` section (mandatory — see §10.4).
3. `### ASSUMPTIONS` section (≤5 bullets).

---

## 6. Deep Dive on Technical Decisions

### 6.1 Tartini parameters (verified Stage 0/1)

Signature: `Tartini.kr(in, ampThreshold=0.01, peakThreshold=0.5, pitchMedianWindow=1, pitchQuant=1, k=1, ...)`

Stage 1 verified `ampThreshold=0.9` works for guitar and voice. Returns `[freq, hasFreq]`. Gate target updates on `hasFreq > 0.5`.

### 6.2 JI pitch class set representation

FrequencySet is a list of **octave-reduced pitch classes** relative to a 220 Hz analysis reference. It is not a fixed chord and not a complete register map.

**Stage 1 verified set (just major scale, 7-limit):**
```
ratios = [1/1, 9/8, 5/4, 4/3, 3/2, 5/3, 15/8]
fundamental = 220.0 Hz
analysis freqs = [220.0, 247.5, 275.0, 293.333, 330.0, 366.667, 412.5]
```

**Octave equivalence:** Ratios are folded into `[1, 2)`. `2/1`, `1/2`, `9/4`, etc. are the same class. Snapping an input frequency `f`:
1. `oct = floor(log2(f / fundamental))`
2. `fOct = f / 2^oct` (folds into `[fundamental, 2*fundamental)`)
3. Find nearest class ratio `r` to `fOct`.
4. Return `r * fundamental * 2^oct`.

The target octave is local to the input. A guitar and a voice can instantiate the same class in different octaves. Live autotune therefore needs **no global register assignment**.

**Size policy:** target ≈7 classes; hard maximum 12; soft floor 3. Twelve is a ceiling, not a goal. Add/remove/replace actions let the set breathe.

**Register vs class:**
- Pitch classes are global.
- Octaves are per voice or per analysis instantiation.
- Two voices playing the same class at different octaves are two voices, one class.
- Roughness is computed on actual voiced frequencies, so it is register-dependent even though class membership is not.
- Stage 2 keeps one representative `analysis_voicing` (class → octave) for scoring. It is not a constraint on live inputs.

**Anchor:** one class is designated the drone/anchor. `anchor_mobility` controls its removal weight: 0.0 = never removed by a mutation, 0.25 = the old 1/4 weight (default), 1.0 = the same weight as any class with random re-anchoring among survivors. Below 1.0, if removed it re-anchors to the nearest surviving class. The 220 Hz analysis reference remains fixed; the anchor class and its octave provide the musical drone.

**Server-side representation:** Python sends the octave-reduced ratio set, the fundamental, and the glide time to sclang over `/ji/freqset/update`. sclang owns the nearest-class lookup (the verified Stage 1 routine) and writes the current Hz values into a 12-slot Buffer mirror for future stages. No SynthDef reads that mirror in Stage 3. A server-side nearest-class SynthDef is deferred.

### 6.3 Nearest-class lookup

**Stage 1 implementation (verified):** sclang reads Tartini freq via `Bus.get` at 20 Hz, does nearest-class search, calls `Synth.set(\targetFreq, ...)`. Control latency ≤50 ms. This works and sounds great. **No change needed.**

**Stage 3 implementation:** Python sends the complete set on every mutation; sclang updates `~ji_ji_set_ratios` / `~ji_ji_fundamental`, stores the pending glide, and reuses the Stage 1 lookup in the input's own octave. When the target changes, sclang sets `retuneTime = max(glide, 0.005)` before setting `targetFreq`, then clears the pending glide.

**Stage 4+ optimization (if needed):** Custom C++ UGen `NearestFreq` at audio rate. Only if control latency becomes audible.

### 6.4 Pitch shifting — `PitchShift` parameters (verified Stage 1)

`PitchShift.ar(in, windowSize=0.2, pitchRatio=1.0, pitchDispersion=0.0, timeDispersion=0.0)`

- `windowSize` 0.1 s verified good default for guitar.
- `pitchRatio` clamped to `[0.25, 4.0]`.
- `Lag.kr` on ratio prevents zipper noise.
- `retuneTime` maps directly to `Lag.kr` time constant.

### 6.5 Mutation algorithm (denominator + roughness, decentralised)

Given set `S` (3 ≤ |S| ≤ 12, target 7) and parameters `randomness ∈ [0,1]`, `dissonanceBias ∈ [-1,1]`, `stepwiseBias ∈ [0,1]`, `temperature ∈ [0,1]`, and batch width `changeCount ∈ [1, maxChanges]`:

**Action selection:** add/remove/replace are weighted by distance from `targetSize`; no action is forced except when at a boundary (add below 3, remove/replace at 12). The set does not fill to 12.

**Batch width:** `changeCount` selects how many sequential edits one trigger applies. `MutationEngine.mutate_many()` generates each edit against the evolving set, applies all of them, and rolls the engine back if any step fails. The bridge sends one final `/ji/freqset/update`, so a batch is one audible retune, not several intermediate sets. `maxChanges` is a constructor/CLI ceiling (default 3, maximum 12); the OSC change-count is clamped to it. Action policy still keeps the set at 3–12.

**Candidate generation:**
- For each current class `r` and each interval in a rational vocabulary, generate `foldOctave(r * q)`.
- At `temperature = 0`, the vocabulary is the Stage 2 `INTERVAL_POOL` and rational neighbours use denominator limit 16.
- Above 0, higher-limit intervals from `EXTENDED_INTERVAL_POOL` (primes 17, 19, 23, 29, 31) are admitted probabilistically, and the rational-neighbour denominator limit grows to 32 at `temperature = 1`.
- Add rational-neighbour candidates derived from current denominators.
- Filter existing classes, near-unisons (<15 cents from surviving classes), and, for stepwise, classes closer than 10 cents or farther than ±1.5 semitones from the reference.
- If generation is empty, an emergency rational scan finds a fresh class. The engine never refuses to mutate.

The old fixed ten-ratio pool is no longer the universe; it remains one source of intervals.

**Scoring:** lower is better.
1. **Denominator complexity across all surviving pairs** (primary, register-invariant): approximate relative ratios as fractions and sum their denominators.
2. **Sethares roughness across all actual voiced frequencies** (secondary texture): `sum(roughness(candidate, r))`.
3. **Anchor term** (small): relation to the drone class.
4. **Near-duplicate penalty** (small).

Temperature relaxes the first three weights by `(1 - 0.5 * temperature)`; the near-duplicate penalty stays fixed. At `temperature = 0` the Stage 2 score is unchanged. The scoring denominator limit also grows with temperature.

**Selection:** sort by score; sample with
```
effectiveRandomness = randomness + temperature * (1 - randomness)
weight[i] = exp(alpha * normalized_rank[i])
alpha = -10 * (1 - effectiveRandomness) + 10 * dissonanceBias
```
The best score is a bias, not a mandate. Positive dissonance bias favours rougher/more complex candidates; negative favours simpler ones. At `temperature = 1` and `dissonanceBias = 0` every rank is equally likely; extended/high-limit classes can win. The engine does not converge to a minimum.

**Removal:** weighted by roughness contribution. The anchor's weight is multiplied by `anchorMobility`:
- `0.0` — anchor never removed or replaced by a mutation; octave moves also avoid it.
- `0.25` — default; the old 1/4 removal weight, so anchor shifts are rare.
- `1.0` — same removal weight as any class; if removed, a random surviving class becomes the new anchor (no positional precedence).
Below 1.0, re-anchoring goes to the class nearest the old anchor. The 220 Hz analysis reference does not drift; only the anchor class moves.

**Octave/register:** separate from class mutation. `OctaveEvent` moves one class in the analysis voicing up/down an octave; `MutationEvent.new_octave` is a hint for MIDI/drone/analysis. Live autotune ignores these hints and uses the input octave.

**Output:**
```
MutationEvent(removedRatio, newRatio, glideTime, reason, newOctave, removedOctave)
reason ∈ {"added", "removed", "replaced", "stepwise"}

MutationBatch(events: tuple[MutationEvent, ...], glideTime)
```
`newRatio=None` for pure removal; `removedRatio=None` for pure addition. A single-change trigger still returns a `MutationEvent`; a batch trigger returns `MutationBatch` with the already-applied edits.

**Manual trigger only.** No auto-clocking. MIDI mapping in Stage 8.

### 6.6 Loop recording and playback

**Stage 4 implementation (mono):** raw input (pre-autotune) into a mono Buffer via `RecordBuf`, `loop: 0`, `doneAction: 2`, overwrite from frame 0. The `run` control and `~ji_stop_record.()` stop early; the buffer is kept. Buffer length is `seconds * sampleRate` (one channel). No overdub.

**Playback:** `PlayBuf.ar` (looping, mono) → `LeakDC` → `Tartini` → `PitchShift` → output. Each loop has its own tracker buses and takes `targetFreq`/`retuneTime` from sclang's lookup, so it retunes to the current set because playback passes through the autotune chain.

**Key property:** Loops retune to the current set because playback passes through the autotune chain. The buffer itself stays dry.

**Deferred:** stereo loop buffers, `~ji_buf_*` `.aiff` persistence via `Buffer.write`/`Buffer.read`, and per-loop output pairs beyond loops 1/2 are later-stage work (Stage 8 for stereo/per-loop routing and persistence formats). Stage 5 provides the 16-channel infrastructure and keeps `test_dual_loop.scd` on pair 0/1; Stage 5.5 gives loops 1/2 dedicated mono channels on SC 2/3.

**Loop length:** `test_live_rig.scd` defaults to **8.0 s** per loop. `~ji_record_loop.(i)` uses `~ji_loop_default_seconds`; `~ji_record_loop.(i, seconds)` overrides one take; `~ji_set_loop_default.(seconds)` changes the default. Every recording reallocates a buffer of exactly the requested length. `test_dual_loop.scd` retains its original 4.0 s default.

### 6.7 VST hosting (Stage 8, optional)

If Ableton hosts VSTs, SC-side VST hosting is unnecessary. **Decision deferred to Stage 8.** If the owner wants VSTs *before* the autotune (i.e., inside SC's input chain), use `VSTPlugin.ar`. If VSTs are only needed downstream (post-autotune), Ableton handles them.

### 6.8 MIDI mutation mapping

Python `mido` listens for note-on. Configurable note → action mapping via `config/midi_map.json`. Actions: `"mutate"`, `"mutate_random"`, `"mutate_stepwise"`. Mappings configurable via OSC `/ji/midi/map [noteNumber, action]`.

### 6.9 Granular FX

`TGrains` or `GrainBuf` with parameters: `grainSize`, `density`, `pitchRandom` (quantized to JI set), `positionJitter`, `reverseProb`, `trigger` (gate).

**Pitch quantization:** Grain pitch snapped to nearest class in current set. Configurable via `quantizeToSet` (default true).

**Gate:** Short fade (10 ms) on transitions to avoid clicks.

### 6.10 Ableton audio routing (Stage 5, implemented)

**Goal:** Send up to 8 independent stereo streams from SC to Ableton, so Ableton hosts per-stream VSTs/instruments and handles final mixing. Stage 5 implements the infrastructure; Stage 4 live/loop audio still occupies pair 0/1.

**Primary setup (human, one-time):**

1. Install **BlackHole 16ch**.
2. In Audio MIDI Setup, set the **MiniFuse 2** and **BlackHole 16ch** to 48000 Hz.
3. No Aggregate Device is needed: SC reads the MiniFuse 2 and writes the
   BlackHole 16ch with separate `inDevice`/`outDevice` settings.
4. Ableton input is BlackHole 16ch with inputs 1–16 enabled; Ableton output is
   the MiniFuse 2/headphones for monitoring.

**SC config (`startup.scd`):**
```supercollider
var inDeviceName  = "MiniFuse 2";
var outDeviceName = "BlackHole 16ch";

s.options.inDevice_("MiniFuse 2");
s.options.outDevice_("BlackHole 16ch");
s.options.numInputBusChannels = 2;
s.options.numOutputBusChannels = 16;  // 8 stereo pairs
s.options.blockSize = 64;
s.options.sampleRate = 0;  // 0 = use the devices' Audio MIDI Setup rate; do not force 48000
```

`startup.scd` verifies both names against `ServerOptions.inDevices` /
`outDevices` (comparing by value), prints the available lists, and prints on
boot:

```text
inDevice=MiniFuse 2 outDevice=BlackHole 16ch
Audio I/O: 2 in / 16 out, 48000 Hz, blockSize 64
```

The 48 kHz rate comes from Audio MIDI Setup, not from a forced
`s.options.sampleRate`. Forcing 48000 with a separate BlackHole output can make
scsynth exit with code 0 after "server ready"; `sampleRate = 0` uses the device
rate and clears any old forced value. The post-boot check warns if the actual
rate is not 48000 Hz. If a name is not found it warns and lists the available
devices; it does not substitute another physical device. A `singleDeviceName`
fallback supports a shared/Aggregate device and is documented in
`README_stage5.md` §9.

**Ableton config:**
- Preferences → Audio → Audio Input Device: BlackHole 16ch.
- Input Config: enable channels 1–16.
- Audio Output Device: MiniFuse 2 (or its headphones); sample rate 48000 Hz.
- Create 8 stereo audio tracks. Track N input = BlackHole channels 2N-1/2N,
  Monitor **In**, Audio To Master.
- Master output → MiniFuse 2 1/2 (or headphones). Do not route the master back
  to BlackHole.

**Test:** `test_ableton_routing.scd` plays a 1 kHz sine on each of the eight
stereo pairs sequentially (about 1 s per pair, with a release and an explicit
free between pairs) and prints:

```text
[ROUTING] pair 1 (Ableton track 1) -> SC ch 0/1
...
[ROUTING] pair 8 (Ableton track 8) -> SC ch 14/15
[ROUTING] done
```

In Ableton, the matching track meters in order and the tone is audible through
the master output. Run a 5-minute drift/dropout check with both devices at
48 kHz.

**Stage 5 scope note:** the routing infrastructure (separate devices, 16 output
buses, all 8 pairs tested) is Stage 5. Stage 4 live/loop audio stays on pair 0/1
(Ableton track 1) for the first sound check. Stage 5.5's live rig gives loops
1/2 dedicated mono channels (SC 2/3); stereo/per-loop pair routing beyond that
is deferred to Stage 8. The Stage 5 map is in §4.2 and
`config/ableton_routing.json`; the live-rig map is §4.2.1 and
`config/live_rig_routing.json`.

**Critical failures to watch:**
- **Device-name mismatch:** use `ServerOptions.inDevices` / `outDevices` for exact names. A typo makes scsynth fall back or fail.
- **No input in Ableton:** Input Config doesn't have channels 1–16 enabled, or the wrong device is selected.
- **No monitoring:** SC writes to BlackHole, which is virtual; Ableton must monitor BlackHole and send its master to the MiniFuse/headphones.
- **Separate-device drift:** MiniFuse 2 and BlackHole have independent clocks; both must be 48 kHz. Test a 5-minute run. An Aggregate Device is the fallback if separate-device mode is unstable.
- **Shared devices:** SC and Ableton use the MiniFuse 2 and BlackHole in opposite directions (SC: MiniFuse input, BlackHole output; Ableton: BlackHole input, MiniFuse output). If CoreAudio/Ableton cannot open them concurrently, or the Live version cannot select separate input/output devices, use the Aggregate fallback documented in `README_stage5.md` §9. Do not make it the primary path.
- **Feedback:** routing Ableton's master or a track back to BlackHole, or monitoring BlackHole twice, causes a loop.

### 6.11 MPE MIDI output (Stage 6, implemented)

**Goal:** the Python control layer sends per-note MPE MIDI to Ableton-hosted
synths, allowing arbitrary JI pitches via pitch bend. Stage 6 is MIDI-only:
there is no new SC audio voice and no new OSC address.

**Two independent buses.** The two outputs are IAC ports, not audio buses and
not MIDI channels:

| Voice | IAC port | Ableton MIDI track |
|---|---|---|
| AnchorDrone | `IAC Driver Bus 1` | `Drone` |
| SetArp | `IAC Driver Bus 2` | `Arp` |

Both streams are monophonic and use member channel 2 (mido channel index 1).
Using channel 2 on both buses is fine because the buses are independent ports.
The MPE master channel 1 is never used for note data.

**MPE convention:** channel 1 (MIDI) is the master channel, global messages
only; channels 2–16 are member channels. Member channel 2 carries note-on,
note-off, and per-note pitch bend.

**Pitch-bend math.** Given target frequency `f` and bend range `R` semitones:

```
n = round(69 + 12 * log2(f / 440))       # nearest MIDI note
d = 12 * log2(f / mtof(n))               # deviation in semitones
bend14 = 8192 + round(d / R * 8192)      # 14-bit value
```

- `n` is clamped to `0..127`; if `f` is outside MIDI range, transpose it by
  whole octaves and use the adjusted frequency for `d`.
- `bend14` is clamped to `0..16383`. With `R = 48` the nearest-note deviation
  is at most half a semitone, so the clamp is a safety net.
- mido uses centered pitch: `pitch = bend14 - 8192`, in `-8192..8191`.
- Send pitchwheel **before** note-on on the same member channel, so the first
  sounding sample is already in tune.
- Default `R = 48` semitones. It must match the instrument's MPE pitch-bend
  range in Ableton; a mismatch makes every JI pitch wrong. If the instrument is
  not MPE-capable it plays 12-TET; do not use it for this test.

**Port discipline.** Exact IAC names are read from `config/mpe_config.json`.
Opening a port calls `mido.get_output_names()`; a missing name raises a clear
error listing the available outputs and never silently uses the other bus.
`mido`/`python-rtmidi` are optional and imported lazily, so the Stage 2/3.5
harnesses still run without mido.

**Implementation.** `control/mpe_output.py` provides `MPEOutput`,
`frequency_to_pitch()`, `MPEPitch`, and `MPEMessage`. A real mido port is wrapped
in an adapter that converts `MPEMessage` to `mido.Message`; the pure tests pass a
fake output object that receives `MPEMessage` and never imports mido.

**Calibration:** see §15.6 and `README_mpe.md`. Both Ableton tracks must play
C4 from `test_mpe.py --calibrate`; a 275 Hz JI note must read 275 Hz, not
277.18 Hz (C#4) or 261.63 Hz (C4).

### 6.12 Anchor drone (Stage 6, implemented)

**Behavior:**
- Monophonic. One MPE output on `IAC Driver Bus 1`, member channel 2.
- **Pitch:** `engine.anchor_ratio * engine.fundamental`, at the fundamental
  octave. Do **not** use the analysis voicing octave. If the frequency is
  outside MIDI range, transpose by whole octaves in the MPE helper.
- **Fallback:** if `anchor_ratio` is `None`, use `current_ratios[0]` and log a
  warning. If the anchor is not in the current set, use the first class and log
  a warning.
- **Start:** send pitch bend, then note-on, then hold the note.
- **Set updates:**
  - anchor frequency unchanged → send nothing; the note stays constant and
    does not retrigger;
  - anchor changed but maps to the same MIDI note → send only a pitch bend;
  - anchor changed to a different MIDI note → note-off the old note, then
    pitch bend + note-on the new note.
- **Stop:** note-off the held note and send CC 123 (All Notes Off).
- **No old policy:** this phase does not select the most consonant class and
  does not wait for N mutations. It follows the anchor directly. There are no
  `/ji/bass/*` OSC addresses.

**Implementation:** `control/drone.py` (`AnchorDrone`). It receives immutable
snapshots via `on_set(ratios, anchor, fundamental)` and never reads the engine.

### 6.13 Set arp (Stage 6, implemented)

**Behavior:**
- Continuous monophonic sequence on `IAC Driver Bus 2`, member channel 2. It is
  regenerated on every set update from the snapshot.
- **Shared ratio:** default is the current anchor if it is in the set; otherwise
  a random class. Configurable (`arp_shared_mode`: `"anchor"` | `"random"`).
- **Groups:** `A = [shared, a1, a2]`, `B = [shared, b1, b2]`.
  - At set sizes 5–12, the four non-shared slots are four distinct classes, so
    A and B share exactly one ratio.
  - Below 5 classes the non-shared pool is reused gracefully; the arp still
    runs, but exact-one-shared/four-distinct is only guaranteed at sizes ≥ 5.
- **Shared identity:** the shared note is constructed once with one ratio, one
  octave offset, and one frequency, and that same note object appears first in
  both groups. Its frequency and octave are identical in A and B by
  construction.
- **Register:** default octave offsets `[0, 1, 2]`: shared at offset 0, the two
  non-shared notes at +1 and +2 octaves, in both groups.
- **Sequence:** `A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3, A1, ...`. Each step note-offs the
  previous note (if held), pitch-bends to the new frequency, then note-ons.
- **Timing:** default 0.25 s per step, 0.20 s gate. This is a placeholder;
  rhythm UI, tempo sync, swing, and sequencing are deferred to Stage 8.
- **Set update:** regenerate groups and octave choices, then apply the new
  pattern at the next step boundary. Do not restart the clock or cut a held
  note.
- **RNG:** a dedicated `random.Random(arp_seed)`; the same seed reproduces the
  arp.
- **Logging:** log the shared ratio and both groups when they change, e.g.
  `[ARP] shared=3/2 (329.63 Hz)  A=[3/2, 1/1, 5/4]  B=[3/2, 5/3, 15/8]`.
- Monophonic only. Polyphonic MPE voice allocation is deferred to Stage 8.

**Implementation:** `control/arp.py` (`SetArp`, `build_groups`, `ArpPattern`,
`ArpNote`). The arp thread owns a `threading.Lock` around the current pattern and
a `threading.Event` for stop.

---

## 7. Data Formats & Interfaces

### 7.1 OSC address map (cumulative)

| Address | Args | Direction | Stage |
|---|---|---|---|
| `/ji/mutation/trigger` | none | Control → Bridge | 2 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/octave` | none | Control → Bridge | 2 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/set_randomness` | float [0,1] | Control → Bridge | 2 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/set_dissonance_bias` | float [-1,1] | Control → Bridge | 2 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/set_stepwise_bias` | float [0,1] | Control → Bridge | 2 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/set_glide_time` | float ≥0 | Control → Bridge | 2 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/set_change_count` | number (clamped 1..max_changes) | Control → Bridge | 3.5 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/set_temperature` | float [0,1] | Control → Bridge | 3.5 ✅ |
| `/ji/mutation/set_anchor_mobility` | float [0,1] | Control → Bridge | 3.5 ✅ |
| `/ji/freqset/update` | [float fundamental, float glide_time, float ratio_1 … ratio_N] | Bridge → sclang | 3 ✅ |
| `/ji/ui/hello` | none | UI → Bridge | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/freqset/changed` | [float fundamental, float anchor (-1 if none), float ratio_1 … ratio_N] | Bridge → UI | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/ui/state` | [float randomness, float dissonance_bias, float stepwise_bias, float glide_time, int change_count, float temperature, float anchor_mobility, int max_changes] | Bridge → UI | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/midi/status` | [int enabled, string drone_port, string arp_port] | Bridge → UI | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/autotune/set` | [int channel (0/1), string param, float value] | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/autotune/state` | [int channel, float retuneTime, float mix, float windowSize, float pitchRangeLimit, float tartiniThreshold, float inputGain] | sclang → UI | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/record` | int index 1..4 | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/record_length` | [int index 1..4, float seconds] | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/stop_record` | int index 1..4 | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/play` | int index 1..4 | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/stop` | int index 1..4 | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/stop_all` | none | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/clear` | int index 1..4 | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/set_default_length` | float seconds | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/loop/state` | [int index, string state] | sclang → UI | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/status/query` | none | UI → sclang | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/cpu` | float (percent) | sclang → UI | 7 🟡 |
| `/ji/midi/map` | [int, string] | Control → Bridge | 8 |
| `/ji/granular/trigger` | [float, float] | Control → Server | 9 |
| `/ji/event/mutation` | [float, float, float[]] | Bridge → Visual | 11 |
| `/ji/event/pitchclass` | [int, int, float] | Bridge → Visual | 11 |
| `/ji/event/grain` | [float, float] | Bridge → Visual | 11 |
| `/ji/event/loop` | [int, string] | Bridge → Visual | 11 |
| `/ji/event/drone` | float (Hz) | Bridge → Visual | 11 |

Stage 3 does **not** send `/ji/mutation/apply` or any message directly to scsynth. The complete set is sent on `/ji/freqset/update`; sclang applies it to the verified nearest-class lookup. A multi-edit batch sends one `/ji/freqset/update` with the final set and one glide time, not intermediate sets. **Stage 7 adds the UI control/status addresses above**: the GUI sends mutation controls to the bridge (9000), autotune/loop controls directly to sclang (57120), and listens for status on 9001. The Stage 3 `/ji/freqset/update` contract and Stage 6 MIDI path are unchanged.

### 7.2 FrequencySet (control-layer object)

```python
{
    "ratios": [1.0, 1.125, 1.25, 1.333, 1.5, 1.666, 1.875],  # octave-reduced
    "fundamental": 220.0,
    "targetSize": 7,
    "minSize": 3,
    "maxSize": 12,
    "anchorRatio": 1.0,
    "analysisVoicing": {1.0: 0, 1.125: 0, 1.25: 0, ...}
}
```

`analysisVoicing` is a representative class → octave map for scoring, not a global register constraint. Live input uses its own octave. The bridge sends octave-reduced ratios plus fundamental and glide to sclang; sclang writes the Hz mirror via `Buffer.setn`. That mirror is not read by any SynthDef in Stage 3.

The runtime event shapes are:

```python
MutationEvent(
    removed_ratio=None | float,
    new_ratio=None | float,
    glide_time=float,
    reason="added" | "removed" | "replaced" | "stepwise",
    new_octave=None | int,
    removed_octave=None | int,
)

MutationBatch(
    events=tuple[MutationEvent, ...],  # already applied
    glide_time=float,
)

OctaveEvent(
    ratio=float,
    old_octave=int,
    new_octave=int,
    glide_time=float,
    reason="octave_up" | "octave_down",
)
```

### 7.3 Loop buffer file format

48 kHz, stereo, 32-bit float, `.aiff`. Naming: `loops/loop_<index>_<timestamp>.aiff`.

### 7.4 MIDI mapping file

`config/midi_map.json`:
```json
{
    "note_60": "mutate",
    "note_62": "mutate_stepwise",
    "note_64": "mutate_random",
    "note_65": "drone_trigger",
    "cc_1": "randomness",
    "cc_2": "dissonance_bias"
}
```

### 7.5 MPE configuration file (Stage 6, implemented)

`config/mpe_config.json`:

```json
{
    "drone_port": "IAC Driver Bus 1",
    "arp_port": "IAC Driver Bus 2",
    "member_channel": 2,
    "master_channel": 1,
    "bend_range_semitones": 48,
    "drone_velocity": 90,
    "arp_velocity": 90,
    "step_seconds": 0.25,
    "gate_seconds": 0.20,
    "arp_octave_offsets": [0, 1, 2],
    "arp_shared_mode": "anchor",
    "arp_seed": 1
}
```

`member_channel` is **1-based**; mido receives `member_channel - 1`. Both MIDI
buses are monophonic and use member channel 2. `bend_range_semitones` must equal
the instrument's MPE pitch-bend range (48 in this rig). `arp_octave_offsets` is
positional `(shared, non_shared_1, non_shared_2)`. `arp_shared_mode` is
`"anchor"` or `"random"`; `arp_seed` makes the arp reproducible. Exact port
names are required; a missing name fails with the available output list.

---

## 8. Stage Specifications

### 8.1 Stage 0 — Skeleton, I/O, Tartini Verification ✅ COMPLETE

**Delivered:** `startup.scd`, `synthdefs/ji_tartini_debug.scd`, `test_tartini.scd`, `README_stage0.md`.

**Verified:** SC 3.15 dev boots, sc3-plugins + Tartini ABI-compatible, 2-ch audio interface functional, buses 0–4 readable, Tartini tracks guitar and voice accurately, passthrough clean.

### 8.2 Stage 1 — Static Autotune, Single Channel ✅ COMPLETE

**Delivered:** `startup.scd` (modified, buses 5–7), `synthdefs/ji_autotune_ch.scd`, `test_autotune.scd`, `README_stage1.md`.

**Verified:** Chromatic scale snaps to 7 JI pitches. `retuneTime` audibly changes glide (0.005 = instant snap, 0.5 = portamento). No clicks at note boundaries. CPU < 20%. **"Sounds great" per project owner.**

### 8.3 Stage 2 — Decentralised Mutation Engine ✅ COMPLETE

**Delivered:** `control/mutation.py` (`MutationEngine`, `MutationEvent`, `OctaveEvent`), `control/osc_server.py` (threaded local OSC listener), `test_mutation.py` (visible demo + seven behavioural tests), `README_stage2.md`.

**Design:** pitch classes are octave-reduced and global; registers are local to voices. The set targets ~7 classes (min 3, max 12), never fills to 12, and never refuses to mutate. Candidates are generated dynamically from the current field × rational intervals and scored primarily by all-pairs denominator complexity, with Sethares roughness as a secondary register-dependent texture term. Selection is stochastic by rank. The anchor/drone is protected but re-anchors if removed. Octave movement is a separate `OctaveEvent` and does not change class membership.

**Acceptance test:** `uv run --with python-osc python test_mutation.py` prints the demo and seven tests, then `ALL TESTS PASSED`.

**Verified:** octave folding/deduplication; size breathing around 7; no dead-end from 12; continuous class drift; stepwise bounds; octave moves without size change; anchor retention/re-anchoring. The harness was run under CPython 3.12 (WASM) in the isolated coding workspace; native macOS acceptance uses `uv`.

**Not in Stage 2:** audio, scsynth, OSC bridge, per-voice registers, MIDI, UI.

### 8.4 Stage 3 — Mutation → Autotune Bridge ✅ COMPLETE

**Goal:** A held live input retunes on the next lookup tick when a mutation changes its nearest class, with the event's glide time.

**Architecture:** Python owns the MutationEngine and sends the complete octave-reduced class set, fundamental, and glide time to sclang on `/ji/freqset/update`. sclang keeps the verified Stage 1 nearest-class lookup, updates `~ji_ji_set_ratios` / `~ji_ji_fundamental`, stores the pending glide, and writes a 12-slot Hz Buffer mirror (not read by any SynthDef in Stage 3). No server-side nearest-class SynthDef is added. Live autotune uses the input's own octave; `new_octave` is not used.

**Deliverables:** `control/ji_bridge.py`, `control/osc_server.py` (public trigger/octave/glide API and callbacks), `test_bridge.scd`, `README_stage3.md`. `synthdefs/ji_freqset_receiver.scd` is deliberately not created; that old server-lookup deliverable is deferred/superseded.

**Acceptance test:** start `startup.scd`, evaluate `test_bridge.scd`, then run `uv run --with python-osc python control/ji_bridge.py --interactive --glide-time 0.15`; hold a note and press `m` until its class is removed/replaced.

1. A sustained note snaps to the current set.
2. A mutation retunes it without replay.
3. The target changes to a member of the new set and stays in the input's octave.
4. The transition follows the event's glide time.
5. Rapid mutations produce no clicks, dropouts, or zipper noise.
6. Set size stays between 3 and 12.
7. `o` moves only the analysis voicing, not the class set or live target.

**Status:** accepted by the project owner — audio and Python↔sclang communication are working. The agent could not hear audio; owner acceptance is the authority.

### 8.4.1 Stage 3 mutation-control extension ✅ COMPLETE — OWNER ALL-PASS 2026-10-30

**Goal:** Make the mutation gesture width, harmonic adventurousness, and anchor freedom controllable without changing the accepted audio path.

**Controls:**
- `change_count` / `max_changes` (`1..12`, default max 3): number of sequential class edits per trigger. `MutationEngine.mutate_many()` applies them atomically and returns a `MutationBatch`; the bridge sends one final `/ji/freqset/update`.
- `temperature` (`0..1`): at 0 the Stage 2 palette/scoring is unchanged; above 0, higher-limit intervals from `EXTENDED_INTERVAL_POOL`, a larger rational-neighbour denominator limit, relaxed complexity/roughness/anchor weights, and flatter rank selection are enabled. At 1 with `dissonance_bias=0`, all extended intervals are available and rank selection is uniform.
- `anchor_mobility` (`0..1`): 0 = the anchor is never removed/replaced by a mutation; 0.25 = old 1/4 removal weight; 1 = the same removal weight as any class and random re-anchoring among survivors. The 220 Hz analysis reference does not drift; the anchor class moves.

**OSC:** `/ji/mutation/set_change_count`, `/ji/mutation/set_temperature`, `/ji/mutation/set_anchor_mobility` (plus the existing Stage 2 addresses).

**CLI:** `--change-count`, `--max-changes`, `--temperature`, `--anchor-mobility`; interactive `c`, `t`, `a`.

**Harness:** `uv run --with python-osc python test_mutation_controls.py` prints `ALL CONTROL TESTS PASSED`. It covers batch width, temperature palette expansion, anchor mobility 0/1, server clamping/batch callbacks, and the bridge's one-update-per-batch behaviour. Python-level acceptance passed; the controls were covered by the owner's all-pass listening report (not separately called out).

### 8.5 Stage 4 — Dual Channel + Raw Looper ✅ COMPLETE — OWNER-CONFIRMED 2026-10-30

**Goal:** Two independent input channels (mic 0, guitar 1). Loop recorder with raw buffers. Loops retune on playback.

**Architecture:** two instances of the verified `\ji_autotune_ch` graph — input 0 → out 0 with buses 5/6, input 1 → out 1 with buses 8/9. There is no `ji_autotune_dual.scd`; a wrapper would duplicate the verified Tartini/PitchShift DSP without adding independence. `\ji_looper_recorder` records the **dry** input (mono `RecordBuf`, no overdub) into `~ji_buf_loop1..4`. `\ji_looper_player` runs `PlayBuf → Tartini → PitchShift`; Tartini analyses the unshifted buffer. Loop tracker buses 11–14 (freq) and 15–18 (confidence) are reserved for four loops; the Stage 4 test exercises loops 1 and 2. `test_dual_loop.scd` installs the same `/ji/freqset/update` receiver and `~ji_nearest_target` arithmetic as Stage 3, runs one 20 Hz lookup for all voices, and keeps a per-voice `lastTarget`. A set update **broadcasts** `retuneTime = max(glide, 0.005)` to every active live/loop synth rather than letting one voice consume a shared pending glide. Live inputs and loops use their own octaves; `new_octave` is not used.

**Deliverables:** `startup.scd` (MODIFIED: buses 8–18, sanity/print), `synthdefs/ji_looper_recorder.scd`, `synthdefs/ji_looper_player.scd`, `test_dual_loop.scd`, `README_stage4.md`. `synthdefs/ji_autotune_ch.scd` is unchanged; `test_bridge.scd` is unchanged.

**Acceptance test:**
1. Both channels autotune independently.
2. Record a raw phrase from input 0 into loop 1; playback retunes when the set changes.
3. Record loop 2 from input 1; both live channels and both loops retune together, each in its own octave.
4. CPU < 60% with 2 live + 2 loops.
5. Loop target changes without restarting playback and follows the event glide.
6. No clicks/dropouts/zipper noise during mutations and loop start/stop.

**Status:** implemented in sclang/SynthDefs; functional loop-index bugs were fixed. **The project owner reported all sound checks passed on 2026-10-30** through the Stage 5.5 live rig. The adjustable loop-length control was added after that report as an enhancement. Acceptance is audible and belongs to the project owner.

**Fallback if CPU fails:** store pitch contour at record time, read during playback. Do not add a server-side nearest-class lookup or C++ UGen without measurements.

### 8.6 Stage 5 — BlackHole 16ch + Ableton Audio Integration ✅ COMPLETE — OWNER-CONFIRMED 2026-10-30

**Goal:** SC reads the **MiniFuse 2** (2 input channels) and writes **BlackHole 16ch** (16 output channels). Ableton receives BlackHole inputs 1–16 on 8 stereo tracks, monitors there, and sends its master to the MiniFuse 2/headphones. No Aggregate Device is required; `ServerOptions.inDevice` / `outDevice` split the devices.

**Current state:** `startup.scd` sets `inDevice_("MiniFuse 2")`, `outDevice_("BlackHole 16ch")`, 2 input / 16 output bus channels, blockSize 64; the 48 kHz rate is set on both devices in Audio MIDI Setup. `s.options.sampleRate` is set to **0** (SC's default = use the device rate), not 48000: forcing 48000 with BlackHole as a separate output device can make scsynth exit with code 0 after "server ready". The script verifies the names against `ServerOptions.inDevices` / `outDevices` (by value, not SC's identity-based `includes`), warns and lists devices on a real mismatch, checks `s.sampleRate` after boot, and prints `inDevice=... outDevice=...` / `Audio I/O: 2 in / 16 out...`. Control buses remain 0–18. Stage 4 live/loop audio remains on SC outputs 0/1 and is the first sound-check path through Ableton track 1. The 16-channel infrastructure, not per-loop routing, is the Stage 5 acceptance target. The reserved map is:
- 0–1: live autotuned stereo mix (Stage 4 current audio)
- 2–3: Loop 1 (reserved, Stage 8)
- 4–5: Loop 2 (reserved, Stage 8)
- 6–7: Loop 3 (reserved, Stage 8)
- 8–9: Loop 4 (reserved, Stage 8)
- 10–11: Granular FX (Stage 9)
- 12–13: dry/diagnostic
- 14–15: reserved

Dedicated stereo/per-loop pair routing beyond Stage 5.5's live rig is deferred to Stage 8; Stage 4 mono loops keep their current pair-0/1 behavior in `test_dual_loop.scd` and do not appear on tracks 2–5 of the 8-pair map. Stage 5.5's `test_live_rig.scd` puts loops 1/2 on SC 2/3 (mono).

**Deliverables:**
- `startup.scd` (modified: separate `inDevice_("MiniFuse 2")` / `outDevice_("BlackHole 16ch")`, 2 input / 16 output bus channels, blockSize 64; 48 kHz set on the devices and checked after boot, with `s.options.sampleRate = 0` (device rate) instead of a forced 48000; device-name validation by value and I/O print; single-device/Aggregate fallback variable).
- `test_ableton_routing.scd` (1 kHz sine on each of the 8 stereo pairs sequentially, ~1 s per pair, `[ROUTING] pair N (Ableton track N) -> SC ch X/Y` lines, explicit stop path, safe to re-run).
- `config/ableton_routing.json` (SC bus ↔ Ableton track map and device names).
- `README_stage5.md` (BlackHole/Ableton setup, monitoring, test procedure, shared-device/Aggregate fallback, 5-minute drift check, failure modes, limitations).
- `README_stage4.md` (UPDATED monitor path; Stage 4 DSP/loop behavior unchanged).
- `design_doc_10.28.10.50` (§3.6, §4.2, §6.10, §8.6, §9, §14).

No Python, OSC, SynthDef, or loop-routing change. `synthdefs/ji_autotune_ch.scd`, `test_bridge.scd`, `test_autotune.scd`, `test_tartini.scd`, and `test_dual_loop.scd` remain unchanged.

**Acceptance test:**
1. `ServerOptions.inDevices` / `outDevices` list the exact MiniFuse 2 and BlackHole 16ch names.
2. `startup.scd` boots with 2 in / 16 out; the post window prints `inDevice=MiniFuse 2 outDevice=BlackHole 16ch` and `Audio I/O: 2 in / 16 out, 48000 Hz, blockSize 64`; buses 0–18 and all SynthDefs are present.
3. `test_ableton_routing.scd` plays a tone on each of the 8 stereo pairs in turn.
4. In Ableton (input BlackHole 16ch, inputs 1–16 enabled, 8 stereo tracks, Monitor In), tracks 1–8 meter the matching pair.
5. The human hears the tone through Ableton's master output to the MiniFuse 2/headphones.
6. No drift/dropouts over a 5-minute run with both devices at 48 kHz.
7. `test_dual_loop.scd` still runs; live autotune is audible through Ableton track 1, completing the deferred Stage 4 sound check. Stage 4 loops also remain on track 1, not tracks 2–5.
8. An Aggregate Device is not used; it remains a documented fallback only.

**Status:** implemented and **owner-confirmed 2026-10-30** (routing and Stage 4 sound check passed). The adjustable loop-length control was added after that report as a live-rig enhancement.

**Critical:** use exact device names, set both devices to 48000 Hz, and remember BlackHole is virtual — SC alone is silent without Ableton's monitor path. Stage 5 does not reroute loops or add per-loop tracks.

### 8.6.1 Stage 5.5 — Live-Rig Integration (2 inputs + 2 loops → 4 outputs) ✅ COMPLETE — OWNER-CONFIRMED 2026-10-30

**Goal:** One playable rig combining the accepted Stage 1/3/4 autotune and loop code with the Stage 5 monitoring path and the Stage 2/3 Python mutation bridge. Four mono outputs on the first four BlackHole channels: two autotuned live inputs and two loop voices.

**Map:**
- SC 0 → Ableton track 1: autotuned MiniFuse 2 input 0 (mic)
- SC 1 → Ableton track 2: autotuned MiniFuse 2 input 1 (guitar)
- SC 2 → Ableton track 3: loop 1 (autotuned playback)
- SC 3 → Ableton track 4: loop 2 (autotuned playback)

**Implementation:** `test_live_rig.scd` is a copy of the accepted Stage 4 orchestration with `~ji_loop_outbus = [2, 3, 2, 3]`, a distinct receiver key (`\ji_freqset_update_live_rig`), and live `[AUTOTUNE ch=...]` status lines enabled. It keeps the Stage 3 `/ji/freqset/update` validation, the 20 Hz multi-voice lookup, broadcast glide, per-voice `lastTarget`, and the Stage 4 loop-index fix. Loops 3/4 remain optional and sum into SC 2/3; loop recording uses input 0 for loops 1/3 and input 1 for loops 2/4. Loop length defaults to 8.0 s; `~ji_record_loop.(i)` uses `~ji_loop_default_seconds`, `~ji_record_loop.(i, seconds)` overrides one take, and `~ji_set_loop_default.(seconds)` changes the default. No SynthDef, Python, or OSC protocol change.

**Deliverables:** `test_live_rig.scd`, `config/live_rig_routing.json`, `README_live_rig.md`; updates to `README_stage5.md`, `README_stage4.md`, and this document.

**Acceptance test:**
1. Stage 5 startup prints `inDevice=MiniFuse 2 outDevice=BlackHole 16ch` and `Audio I/O: 2 in / 16 out, 48000 Hz, blockSize 64`.
2. `test_live_rig.scd` starts two live autotune voices, the set receiver, four loop buffers, and the 20 Hz lookup; prints `[RIG] Ready`.
3. Ableton has four mono tracks from BlackHole inputs 1, 2, 3, 4, Monitor In, master to MiniFuse 2. Tracks 1/2 carry the live autotuned inputs; tracks 3/4 carry loops 1/2.
4. Python bridge runs; `m` sends the complete set; live and playing loops retune without replay.
5. Loop 1 records input 0 and plays on SC 2/track 3; loop 2 records input 1 and plays on SC 3/track 4. The default length is 8 s; `~ji_record_loop.(1, 16.0)` and `~ji_set_loop_default.(12.0)` change it.
6. CPU < 60% with 2 live + 2 loops.
7. No clicks/dropouts during mutations or loop start/stop.
8. The human hears all four channels through Ableton and can confirm the deferred Stage 1/3.5/4/5 sound checks.

**Status:** implemented and **owner-confirmed 2026-10-30** (all sound checks passed). The adjustable loop-length control (8 s default, per-take override, `~ji_set_loop_default`) was added after that report; a quick re-check of the length control is recommended.

### 8.7 Stage 6 — MPE MIDI (Anchor Drone + Set Arp) ✅ COMPLETE — OWNER-CONFIRMED 2026-10-30

**Goal:** the Python control layer sends two independent monophonic MPE MIDI
streams to Ableton: an anchor-following drone on `IAC Driver Bus 1` and a
two-group set arp on `IAC Driver Bus 2`. No new SC audio voice, no new OSC
address, no rhythm UI, no MIDI mutation mapping, and no Ableton API automation.

**Deliverables:**
- `control/mpe_output.py` — `MPEOutput`, pitch math, port adapter.
- `control/drone.py` — `AnchorDrone`.
- `control/arp.py` — `SetArp`, `build_groups`.
- `control/midi_rig.py` — `MIDIRig`, config loading/validation.
- `control/ji_bridge.py` — optional `--midi` / `--mpe-config`.
- `config/mpe_config.json` — buses, channels, bend range, timing, seed.
- `test_mpe.py` — pure tests plus `--ports`, `--calibrate`, `--run`.
- `README_mpe.md` — IAC/Ableton setup, calibration, failures, acceptance.

**Commands:**
```bash
cd ji-live
python test_mpe.py --pure
uv run --with mido --with python-rtmidi python test_mpe.py --ports
uv run --with mido --with python-rtmidi python test_mpe.py --calibrate
uv run --with mido --with python-rtmidi python test_mpe.py --run 15

uv run --with python-osc python control/ji_bridge.py --interactive --glide-time 0.15
uv run --with python-osc --with mido --with python-rtmidi \
  python control/ji_bridge.py --interactive --midi --glide-time 0.15
```

**Behavior:** see §6.11–§6.13. The drone follows the anchor and does not
retrigger on an unchanged frequency; the arp alternates two three-note groups
that share exactly one ratio at set sizes ≥ 5.

**Status:** owner-confirmed 2026-10-30. The human reported the drone and arp
sounding great through Ableton, both IAC buses working, and no stuck notes; the
existing four-channel audio rig and `/ji/freqset/update` behavior were
unchanged.

**Acceptance test (human, completed 2026-10-30):**
1. `mido.get_output_names()` lists `IAC Driver Bus 1` and `IAC Driver Bus 2`;
   `--ports` passes.
2. Ableton has Drone (Bus 1) and Arp (Bus 2) MIDI tracks, both Monitor In, both
   with MPE-capable instruments and a 48-semitone MPE bend range. Set each
   track's **MIDI From channel selector** to **MPE** or **All Channels**, not
   **Ch. 1**: note data is on MIDI member channel 2, while channel 1 is the
   unused MPE master.
3. `--calibrate` plays C4 on both tracks.
4. With the Stage 5.5 live rig running, `ji_bridge.py --midi` plays a constant
   anchor drone and an alternating two-group arp.
5. Pressing `m` updates the set; the drone follows the anchor (or stays put if
   unchanged), and the arp regenerates with one shared frequency.
6. Quitting sends note-off/CC 123 on both buses; no stuck notes.
7. The existing four-channel audio and `/ji/freqset/update` behavior are
   unchanged.
8. **Owner-confirmed 2026-10-30:** the drone and arp were heard through
   Ableton, MIDI connectivity/calibration was accepted, and no stuck notes were
   observed. The agent's pure tests alone do not claim MIDI/Ableton acceptance;
   the human report is the authority.

### 8.8 Stage 7 — Minimal Performance GUI 🟡 BUILT — AWAITING HUMAN ACCEPTANCE

**Goal:** one barebones PyQt6 window that controls the existing live rig and
mutation/MIDI system. Stage 7 is a control surface, not a new audio engine, not
a rhythm sequencer, and not an aesthetics project.

**Naming:** completed stages 0–6 keep their numbers. The GUI is Stage 7 because
Stage 6 is the accepted MIDI stage; reusing "Stage 6" would break every existing
reference. The names are stable; the numbers are bookkeeping.

**Implementation (2026-10-30):** `ui/osc_control.py` holds the pure message
builders, range/clamp helpers, status parsers, `StatusStore`, `OSCController`,
and the background UDP listener. `ui/ji_ui.py` is one window with Mutation,
Live Autotune, Loops, and Status sections; knob sends are coalesced to ≈30 Hz,
buttons send immediately, and incoming state updates widgets with signals
blocked. `ui/control_surface.scd` adds `/ji/autotune/set`, the eight loop
addresses, `/ji/status/query`, and the CPU/loop/autotune status stream; it
whitelists and clamps the six autotune Synth arguments and calls the existing
`~ji_*` loop functions. "Stop All Loops" uses `~ji_stop_record`/`~ji_stop_loop`
for indices 1–4, not `~ji_stop_all` (which would also free the live autotune
synths and stop the lookup/CPU routines). `control/osc_server.py` adds the additive
`/ji/ui/hello` callback; `control/ji_bridge.py` adds `--ui-host`/`--ui-port`
and sends `/ji/freqset/changed`, `/ji/ui/state`, and `/ji/midi/status` after
every set and on hello. The `/ji/freqset/update` payload and receiver are
unchanged. `test_ui.py` covers the pure layer and an optional offscreen Qt
smoke test; `README_stage7.md` documents setup, start order, ranges, OSC,
failure modes, and acceptance.

**Loop-state timing:** record/re-record allocates the buffer asynchronously, so
sclang reports `recording` about 0.1 s after the action. Stop/Play/Stop/Clear
report immediately; end-of-buffer auto-stop is reported by the 2 Hz status
routine. Clear stops the recorder and player, frees the buffer after the 30 ms
fade, and sets `empty`.

**Verification status:** `test_ui.py --pure`, `test_mutation.py`,
`test_mutation_controls.py`, and `test_mpe.py --pure` pass in the agent
environment. The offscreen Qt test was skipped there because the container
lacks Qt system libraries (e.g. `libEGL.so.1`), and no audio/MIDI/Ableton/live
GUI behavior was testable by the agent. The owner ran the offscreen Qt smoke
test on **2026-10-30** and it passed (four groups, knob/button sends, loop
indices, status sync, waiting labels, clean close), which validates window
construction and signal wiring only. No audio/MIDI/Ableton/live behavior has
been verified yet; Stage 7 therefore awaits the human acceptance check in §11
of `README_stage7.md`.

**Architecture:**

```
UI (PyQt6, one window)
 ├─ OSC 9000  → ji_bridge.py → mutation engine + /ji/freqset/update
 ├─ OSC 57120 → sclang (live autotune + loop transport)
 └─ OSC 9001  ← sclang (CPU, loop state, autotune state)
               ← bridge (frequency set, bridge state, MIDI status)
```

- The UI never talks to Ableton or scsynth directly.
- The UI does not start/stop the audio or MIDI rig; it is a controller.
- OSC receives happen in a background thread; Qt widgets update via signals or
  a `QTimer`, never directly from the OSC thread.
- Existing audio/MIDI contracts are unchanged.

**Window:** one window, grouped controls, no tabs, no menus, no theming pass.
Use `QDial` for knobs, `QPushButton` for actions, labels for status.

**Controls:**
- **Mutation:** Mutate, Octave; knobs for randomness, dissonance bias, stepwise
  bias, temperature, anchor mobility, change count, glide time.
- **Live autotune:** channels 0/1 (the two live inputs); knobs for `retuneTime`,
  `mix`, `windowSize`, `pitchRangeLimit`, `tartiniThreshold`, `inputGain`; a
  Link toggle sends a change to both channels.
- **Loops:** loops 1–4, each with Record, Stop Rec, Play, Stop; a Stop All
  button; a default/take length knob (1–30 s, default 8 s); Clear.
- **Status:** current ratios and Hz, anchor, fundamental, bridge control state,
  CPU, loop states, and MIDI enabled/ports.
- Loop-player autotune knobs, extra MIDI voices, rhythm, save/recall, more
  loops, granular, and visual features are **not** in Stage 7.

**OSC:** see §7.1 for the exact Stage 7 addresses. Range validation belongs in
both the UI and sclang; invalid values must be clamped or rejected with a log
line, never applied as an unsafe Synth argument.

**Deliverables:** `ui/__init__.py`, `ui/ji_ui.py`, `ui/osc_control.py`,
`ui/control_surface.scd`, `test_ui.py`, `README_stage7.md`; additive
UI-status support in `control/ji_bridge.py` and `control/osc_server.py`.

**Tests:** `test_ui.py --pure` with a fake OSC client and no PyQt6;
`test_ui.py --qt` with `QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen` when PyQt6 is available;
existing Stage 2/3.5/6 tests must still pass.

**Acceptance:** the human can drive the listed parameters from one window,
status updates live, loop transport/length works, no clicks or stuck notes, the
Stage 6 MIDI path still works, and closing the UI leaves the audio/MIDI rig
running. Record the result. Stage 7 is not complete until the human confirms.

### 8.9 Stage 8 — Performance Expansion & Iteration (long-running) ⬜ PLANNED

**Goal:** after the minimal GUI exists, iterate on artist capability in small,
shippable increments. This is not a single waterfall stage; each increment gets
its own human listening check and GUI extension.

**Planned increments (priority may change):**
- **More MIDI outputs/voices:** drums, bass, lead, and other parts on
  independent IAC buses/channels; per-voice configuration and enable/disable.
- **Rhythm controls:** tempo, time signature, syncopation, step patterns, and
  editable arp/MIDI generation timing. The Stage 6 fixed 0.25 s/0.20 s arp is a
  placeholder.
- **State save/recall:** save/load frequency sets, mutation parameters, MIDI
  voice patterns/settings, and loop settings as JSON; recall must not require a
  restart.
- **More loops and loop options:** more than four loops, per-loop length,
  overdub/clear, gain, and routing options.
- **MIDI generation controls:** arp group/shared/offset/seed/velocity controls,
  drone behavior, and per-voice pattern parameters exposed in the GUI.
- **Loop-player autotune controls:** the loop-player parameters deferred from
  Stage 7.
- **Optional:** MIDI-triggered mutation mapping and SC-side VST hosting if the
  owner wants them.

**Constraints:** preserve `/ji/freqset/update`, the Stage 5.5 four-channel audio
map, and the Stage 6 MIDI contracts. Extend the GUI incrementally; no big-bang
rewrite.

**Deliverables and acceptance criteria are defined per increment.**

### 8.10 Stage 9 — Granular & Glitch FX ⬜ PLANNED

**Goal:** use the SuperCollider environment for the granular/glitch half of the
project: `TGrains`/`GrainBuf` processing, pitch quantization to the current
class set, gate/fade handling, and GUI controls for the useful parameters.

**Deliverables:** `synthdefs/ji_granular_fx.scd`, `test_granular.scd`, GUI
controls, `README_stage9.md`.

**Acceptance:** audible granular processing on the live/loop material, grains
quantized to the current set, no clicks at gate boundaries, CPU within the live
budget, and the existing autotune/loop/MIDI paths unchanged.

### 8.11 Stage 10 — GUI UX & Aesthetics ⬜ PLANNED

**Goal:** once the feature set has stabilized, improve the single-window GUI's
usability and visual identity: layout, grouping, labels, colour/typography,
performable control sizes, status feedback, and low-risk workflow refinements.
No new DSP or MIDI-generation features are introduced in this stage unless they
are prerequisites for usability.

**Deliverables:** revised `ui/ji_ui.py`, optional reusable widgets, `README_stage10.md`.

**Acceptance:** the owner can perform a full improvisation without terminal
commands or sclang snippets; no audio/MIDI regressions; the UI remains
responsive under live load.

### 8.12 Stage 11 — Visual Half Groundwork (deferred) ⬜ DEFERRED

**Goal:** lay the groundwork for a separate visual process: define and emit OSC
events for mutation, pitch class, drone, loop, and grain state. No embedded
rendering and no visual process inside the audio path.

**Deliverables:** event definitions in the bridge/control surface,
`test_events.py` (OSC listener), and a visual-side interface document.

**Acceptance:** events arrive within the documented latency, a separate visual
process can subscribe without touching audio, and the audio/MIDI rig remains
unchanged when no visual subscriber is present.

